  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1116 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis shows a stationary front stretching from\nthe ArkLaTex into central Mississippi and South Carolina, with a\n1022 mb high over the Mississippi and Ohio Valleys. Radar shows\ndiminishing showers along the front, while dry conditions prevail\nelsewhere across the Mid-South. Water vapor imagery highlights\nbroad eastern CONUS troughing and an Oklahoma Panhandle MCS,\nkeeping the Mid-South in a northwest flow pattern.\n\nOn Friday, the front will lift north as a warm front but is\nexpected to stall near the MS/TN border through the evening,\nheavily influencing local convective potential. Remnants of the\nOklahoma MCS will drift in from the northwest. While high-\nresolution models show potential for showers and isolated storms\nalong and north of I-40, weak upper-level forcing and limited\ninstability dampen confidence. The highest development potential\nremains along the stalled warm front in northern Mississippi.\n\nConvective activity increases Saturday with another approaching\nMCS. Impacts are most likely north of I-40; however, timing and\nevolution depend on a building subtropical ridge. If this ridge\nstrengthens, it may act to suppress or deflect convective\nactivity to the north and east of our region.\n\nAs the warm front clears late Saturday morning, heat and humidity\nwill build. West of the Mississippi River, heat indices may reach\n105 degrees, necessitating a potential Heat Advisory if cloud\ncover remains limited.\n\nBy early next week, the subtropical ridge centers over the Lower\nMississippi Valley, initiating a multi-day period of extreme\nheat. Ensemble guidance (LREF/NBM) indicates high confidence in\ntriple-digit temperatures and heat indices exceeding 105 degrees\nthrough mid-week. A significant, multi-day heat wave is becoming\nincreasingly likely for the Mid-South.\n\nAC3",
